Q: How do I &quot;stamp&quot; my Texture onto my destination? A: <p>Using <code>pos</code> for the <code>texCoord</code> will just sample the texture based on where the pixel is on the screen. If you want the same triangular section of the texture for all triangles, then for each triangle, the <code>texCoord</code> needs to be independent of <code>pos</code>.</p>
<p>In your vertex buffer, you probably want to pass <code>texCoord</code> individually as well as the position:</p>
<pre><code>struct VertexIn {
    float2 position;
    float2 texCoord;
};

vertex VertexOut vertex_main(const device VertexIn* vertexBuffer [[buffer(0)]],
                             uint vid [[vertex_id]]) {
    VertexOut out;
    VertexIn in = vertexBuffer[vid];
    out.position = float4(in.position, 0, 1);
    out.texCoord = in.texCoord;
    return out;
}
</code></pre>
<p>But then, I am not how you define your vertex buffer the CPU side, but then you want to add floats for the <code>texCoords</code> after each pair of existing position floats. For each of your 18 vertices (or 7 unique vertices if you are using indexing) define the texture coordinates something like this:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/nuDWSI3P.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/nuDWSI3P.png" alt="texCoords example" /></a></p>
<p>So on the Swift side instead of (presumably):</p>
<pre><code>let vertexBuffer: [Float] = [
    x0, y0,
    x1, y1,
    ...
]
</code></pre>
<p>Do something like:</p>
<pre><code>let vertexBuffer: [Float] = [
    x0, y0, 0, 1
    x1, y1, 1, 0
    ...
]
</code></pre>
<p>But match the vertex to the corresponding <code>texCoord</code> value in the image.</p>
<p>But I am using (0, 0), (0, 1), and (1, 0) as an example. Getting your desired result depends on how your texture file is laid out. Swap out 0 and 1 with other  values between 0 and 1 until you select the correct region of the texture file.</p>
